<div><img src="https://media.stores.com/mediocre/image/upload/c_pad,f_auto,h_300,q_auto,w_300/cu38py2zfmznbgfodiya.png" /></div><ul>
<li>Vapor Clean uses a water-based extinguisher formula</li>
<li>Dry Stop uses a powder-based extinguisher formula</li>
<li>Both claim to be effective on grease, paper, fabric, wood, and electrical fires</li>
<li>Dry Stop can additionally be used for flammable gas fires</li>
<li>Not a replacement for a full-sized extinguisher</li>
<li>Can they make a margarita: It’s not advisable</li>
</ul><h1>Stay Safe</h1><p>When we sold these previously, <a href="/@Rhyvangaralian">@Rhyvangaralian</a> summed things up very concisely:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If nothing else, this is cheap peace of mind for the kitchen.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Couldn’t agree more. Because even the seasoned kitchen pro is going to have an oopsie occasionally.</p>
<p>Like, for example, <a href="/@PooltoyWolf">@PooltoyWolf</a>’s mom:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I stopped Mom three seconds before she poured water all over a grease fire in a wok, and used a standard fire extinguisher instead. Thank goodness I’d been in the kitchen!</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Both of these–the dry stop or the vapor clean–claim they would’ve worked for that.</p>
<p>And it would’ve worked for <a href="/@Manraya">@Manraya</a> as well:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Using the oven for your toaster oven storage is no good if you forget to check before preheating the oven. My oven caught my toaster oven on fire. Oops.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Huh…</p>
<p>So… wait a minute…</p>
<p>You were storing your toaster… in your oven?</p>
<p>Anyway, let’s just move on.</p>
<p>Now, some suggested these would be good to have in your car. But <a href="/@craigcush">@craigcush</a> set everyone straight on that idea:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Can tell no firefighters commented yet so here goes. These are 21 oz, not enough for a car fire, a 5 pounder will slow down a car fire, I carry a 10# in my car and 4 20 pounders throughout my home. […] Highway patrol carries 15 pound to buy time until the engine company arrives. These won’t stop lithium.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>In other words, this is really for pretty small fires. Like the ones that happen in the kitchen.</p>
<p>And sure, you should <em>have</em> a full-sized fire extinguisher in your kitchen already, but you never know when you’re going to need a little more, right?</p>
<p>Sorry, hate to return to this, but: <a href="/@Manraya">@Manraya</a>, you were using your OVEN as a place to stash your TOASTER OVEN?! Now, while I can appreciate the Oven-ception happening here, and can understand that many apartments and homes have limited counter space and kitchen storage… that just cannot possibly be the best place to put a toaster.</p>
<p>Anyway, getting back on topic, if you’re looking for a place to use these that’s not the car (because they’re not powerful enough) or the kitchen (because you already have a fire extinguisher there), how about outside?</p>
<p>When you’re camping, for example. Or working at the grill. Even <a href="/@craigcush">@craigcush</a> admits (smirkingly) that they’d work for this:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>These might put out your hamburgers that are on fire on the barbecue, but who wants powdered burgers?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Just a word to the wise (<a href="/@Manraya">@Manraya</a>): just make sure there’s nothing stored under the grill grate before you fire it up.</p>